class:: RandID
summary:: Set the synth's random generator ID.
related:: Classes/RandSeed
categories::  UGens>Generators>Stochastic, UGens>Random


Description::

Choose which random number generator to use for this synth.
All synths that use the same generator reproduce the same sequence
of numbers when the same seed is set again.

In SC 3.15.x and later, code::RandID.kr:: will set the random number generator ID on every control block. This is to support the use of different RNG IDs for different parts of the same SynthDef. See link::#Multiple random number generators:: below.

classmethods::

method::kr, ir

argument::id
The random number generator ID.


Examples::

code::

// start a noise patch and set the id of the generator
(
SynthDef("help-RandID", { |out = 0, id = 1|
	RandID.ir(id);
	Out.ar(out,
		WhiteNoise.ar(0.05) + Dust2.ar(70)
	)
}).add;
)

// reset the seed of my rgen at a variable rate
(
SynthDef("help-RandSeed", { |seed = 1910, id = 1|
	RandID.kr(id);
	RandSeed.kr(Impulse.kr(FSinOsc.kr(0.2, 0, 10, 11)), seed);
}).add;

)

// start two noise synths on left and right channel with a different randgen id
a = Synth("help-RandID", [\out, 0, \id, 1]);
b = Synth("help-RandID", [\out, 1, \id, 2]);

// reset the seed of randgen 1
x = Synth("help-RandSeed", [\id, 1]);

// change the target randgen to 2 (affects right channel)
x.set(\id, 2);
::

subsection:: Multiple random number generators

In most cases, randomized UGens in a SynthDef may all pull from the same random number generator. In this typical case, it's likely unnecessary to set the ID or re-seed at all.

If, however, one needs individual control over separate random-number streams, where each stream is obtained from a different random number generator, then the following sequence of evaluation is needed:

numberedlist::
## Set RNG ID.
## Calculate some randomized UGens.
## Set a different RNG ID.
## Calculate some other randomized UGens.
::

This entire sequence needs to be performed, in this order, on every control block.

note:: code::RandID.ir:: will always evaluate only once, at the start of the synth. If you are using multiple random number generators in the same synth, you must use code::RandID.kr::. ::

As of SC 3.15.x, RandID sets the ID on every control block, supporting the above case transparently. This example uses two identically seeded random number generators, ID 0 and ID 1, and subtracts them to demonstrate that both generators are producing equivalent results (as they should, if they are seeded with the same value). SC 3.14.x and earlier would set the ID only when it changed; this case was not supported in those earlier versions.

code::
(
{
	var a, b;
	RandID.kr(0);
	RandSeed.ir(1, 88372);
	a = LFDNoise0.ar(440);
	RandID.kr(1);
	RandSeed.ir(1, 88372);
	b = LFDNoise0.ar(440);
	[a, b, a - b]
}.plot;
)
::

note:: In such cases, use a SynthDef and check the order of UGens carefully.

code::
(
SynthDef(\test, {
	var a, b;
	RandID.kr(0, 1);
	RandSeed.ir(1, 88372);
	a = LFDNoise0.ar(440);
	RandID.kr(1, 1);
	RandSeed.ir(1, 88372);
	b = LFDNoise0.ar(440);
	Out.ar(1000, [a, b, a - b])
}).dumpUGens
)

// correct order! annotated:

// one RNG chain, id = 0
[0_RandID, control, [0, 1]]
[1_RandSeed, scalar, [1, 88372]]
[2_LFDNoise0, audio, [440]]

// another RNG chain, id = 1
[3_RandID, control, [1, 1]]
[4_RandSeed, scalar, [1, 88372]]
[5_LFDNoise0, audio, [440]]

[6_-, audio, [2_LFDNoise0, 5_LFDNoise0]]
[7_Out, audio, [1000, 2_LFDNoise0, 5_LFDNoise0, 6_-]]
::

::
